<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Collision Checking with Learned Critics</h3>

<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08157v1/check_collision_figure.svg" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:558/258;" width="558" height="258"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Continuous-time collision checking between two agents on spatially intersecting edges. We geometrically determine the minimum separation distance, <math id="A1.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>, between the agents, each with radius <math id="A1.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> using the learning distance critic. This corresponds to minimizing the squared distance function, <math id="A1.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, over the time interval <math id="A1.F6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. A collision is detected if <math id="A1.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{f(\tau_{\rm clamped})}=d_{\min}\leq 2r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>clamped</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{f(\tau_{\rm clamped})}=d_{\min}\leq 2r</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To detect the collisions illustrated in Figure <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.1 Collision Checking with Learned Critics ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Risk-Bounded Multi-Agent Visual Navigation via Dynamic Budget Allocation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we model each agent as a disc of fixed radius <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. When two agents traverse distinct graph edges, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{0},p_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{0},p_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{0},q_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{0},q_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, we define their positions over normalized time <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> as linear interpolations:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p_{0}+\tau(p_{1}-p_{0})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p_{0}+\tau(p_{1}-p_{0}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q_{0}+\tau(q_{1}-q_{0})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q_{0}+\tau(q_{1}-q_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The squared distance between the agents’ centers is a quadratic function of time, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\tau)=\lVert P(\tau)-Q(\tau)\rVert^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\tau)=\lVert P(\tau)-Q(\tau)\rVert^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. By defining the initial relative offset <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}=p_{0}-q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}=p_{0}-q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the relative velocity <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}=(p_{1}-p_{0})-(q_{1}-q_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}=(p_{1}-p_{0})-(q_{1}-q_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, we can write this function in its standard form:</p>
<table id="A1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\tau)=\lVert\mathbf{D}+\tau\mathbf{V}\rVert^{2}=a\tau^{2}+b\tau+c," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\tau)=\lVert\mathbf{D}+\tau\mathbf{V}\rVert^{2}=a\tau^{2}+b\tau+c,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where the coefficients are <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\mathbf{V}\cdot\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\mathbf{V}\cdot\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=2\mathbf{D}\cdot\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐃</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=2\mathbf{D}\cdot\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\mathbf{D}\cdot\mathbf{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐃</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\mathbf{D}\cdot\mathbf{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Coefficients from Learned Distances.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our objective is to compute these coefficients using only distance queries, which can be provided by our learned distance critic. The constant term, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, is trivially the initial squared distance: <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\lVert p_{0}-q_{0}\rVert^{2}=\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},q_{0})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\lVert p_{0}-q_{0}\rVert^{2}=\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},q_{0})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To express <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, we use a vector identity derived from the law of cosines to replace dot products with squared distances. The key identity for two vectors <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2(\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2(\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{dist}(p_{i},q_{i})^{2}+\mathrm{dist}(p_{j},q_{j})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{dist}(p_{i},q_{i})^{2}+\mathrm{dist}(p_{j},q_{j})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad-\mathrm{dist}(p_{i},q_{j})^{2}-\mathrm{dist}(p_{j},q_{i})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad-\mathrm{dist}(p_{i},q_{j})^{2}-\mathrm{dist}(p_{j},q_{i})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=p_{i}-p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=p_{i}-p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}=q_{i}-q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}=q_{i}-q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. By systematically applying this identity to the dot products within the expressions for <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\mathbf{V}\cdot\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\mathbf{V}\cdot\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=2\mathbf{D}\cdot\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐃</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=2\mathbf{D}\cdot\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, we arrive at their final forms:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},p_{1})^{2}+\mathrm{dist}(q_{0},q_{1})^{2}+\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},q_{0})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},p_{1})^{2}+\mathrm{dist}(q_{0},q_{1})^{2}+\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},q_{0})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\mathrm{dist}(p_{1},q_{1})^{2}-\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},q_{1})^{2}-\mathrm{dist}(p_{1},q_{0})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\mathrm{dist}(p_{1},q_{1})^{2}-\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},q_{1})^{2}-\mathrm{dist}(p_{1},q_{0})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{dist}(p_{1},q_{0})^{2}-\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},p_{1})^{2}-2\,\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},q_{0})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{dist}(p_{1},q_{0})^{2}-\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},p_{1})^{2}-2\,\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},q_{0})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad-\mathrm{dist}(q_{0},q_{1})^{2}+\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},q_{1})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad-\mathrm{dist}(q_{0},q_{1})^{2}+\mathrm{dist}(p_{0},q_{1})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Minimizer and Collision Test.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the coefficients computed, we find the time of closest approach. For <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the unconstrained minimizer of the quadratic is <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau^{*}=-b/(2a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{*}=-b/(2a)</annotation></semantics></math>. We clamp this value to the valid time interval to get <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\rm clamped}=\min(1,\max(0,\tau^{*}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>clamped</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\rm clamped}=\min(1,\max(0,\tau^{*}))</annotation></semantics></math>. A collision is detected if the minimum distance is less than or equal to the sum of the radii:</p>
<table id="A1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{f(\tau_{\rm clamped})}\leq 2r." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>clamped</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{f(\tau_{\rm clamped})}\leq 2r.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Crucially, this entire continuous-time collision check is performed using only the outputs of the learned distance critic, seamlessly integrating it into our planning framework with unstructured graphs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>More Results</h3>

<section id="A1.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Ablation Study</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Ablation Study ‣ A.2 More Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Risk-Bounded Multi-Agent Visual Navigation via Dynamic Budget Allocation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> presents an ablation study on the initial risk allocation strategies for RB-CBS. The results are shown for the 2D Point environment and challenging, high-fidelity visual navigation environments on three distinct scenes. The findings are remarkably consistent across all environments. The Constant allocation strategy (blue) involves an initial allocation of risk budgets that is not dynamically adjusted by the planner. This approach consistently proves to be brittle. It achieves very low or zero success rates at tight risk budgets (<math id="A1.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\leq 25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\leq 25\%</annotation></semantics></math>) because its rigid, pre-set allocation cannot adapt when one or more agents require a larger share of the risk budget to find a valid path.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, the Uniform (orange), Utility-based (green) and Inverse Utility-based (red) strategies demonstrate the power of dynamic risk allocation. They maintain high success rates across nearly all risk levels and environments. Crucially, they exhibit the desired adaptive behavior where at <math id="A1.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\underline{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\underline{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, they accept longer paths (higher steps) to ensure safety (lower cost), and at <math id="A1.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\overline{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\overline{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, they prioritize shorter paths. The similar performance between these three strategies, even in complex visual scenes, suggests that the simplicity of uniform allocation is highly effective. This comprehensive result validates our choice to use the Uniform strategy in the main paper’s analysis, as it provides the benefits of dynamic allocation without the overhead of a the utility computation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08157v1/pointenv_ablation_hard_10_svg-tex.svg" id="A1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/206;" width="698" height="206"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08157v1/habitatenv_ablation_sc2_staging_08_hard_10_svg-tex.svg" id="A1.F7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/206;" width="698" height="206"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08157v1/habitatenv_ablation_sc0_staging_20_hard_10_svg-tex.svg" id="A1.F7.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/206;" width="698" height="206"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08157v1/habitatenv_ablation_sc3_staging_11_hard_10_svg-tex.svg" id="A1.F7.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/206;" width="698" height="206"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>
<span id="A1.F7.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation Study on Initial Risk Allocation Strategies.</span>
Comparison of <span id="A1.F7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Constant</span>, <span id="A1.F7.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Uniform</span>, <span id="A1.F7.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Utility-based</span>, and <span id="A1.F7.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Inverse Utility-based</span> risk allocation for RB-CBS across four distinct environments (10 Agents, Hard Difficulty). The results consistently demonstrate that dynamic allocation strategies (Uniform, Utility and Inverse Utility) far outperform the rigid Constant approach, especially at tight risk budgets (<math id="A1.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\underline{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\underline{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>). Higher success rates, lower steps and lower costs are better.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Point Environment</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now present the complete experimental results for the simpler 2D Point Navigation environment, detailing performance across Easy, Medium, and Hard difficulties with both 5 and 10 agents. Figures <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Point Environment ‣ A.2 More Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Risk-Bounded Multi-Agent Visual Navigation via Dynamic Budget Allocation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#A1.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Point Environment ‣ A.2 More Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Risk-Bounded Multi-Agent Visual Navigation via Dynamic Budget Allocation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> provides a visual summary of these results, while Tables <a href="#A1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Point Environment ‣ A.2 More Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Risk-Bounded Multi-Agent Visual Navigation via Dynamic Budget Allocation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Point Environment ‣ A.2 More Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Risk-Bounded Multi-Agent Visual Navigation via Dynamic Budget Allocation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> offer the precise numerical data.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across all difficulties and agent counts in the 2D Point Environment, our method, RB-CBS, consistently demonstrates superior flexibility and performance. As shown in Figures <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Point Environment ‣ A.2 More Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Risk-Bounded Multi-Agent Visual Navigation via Dynamic Budget Allocation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#A1.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Point Environment ‣ A.2 More Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Risk-Bounded Multi-Agent Visual Navigation via Dynamic Budget Allocation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> , RB-CBS (purple) exhibits the most desirable adaptive behavior. At tight risk budgets (<math id="A1.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\underline{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\underline{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>), it prioritizes safety, achieving low cumulative costs, often at the expense of taking more steps than balanced methods like Bi-Objective Search. As the risk budget is relaxed to <math id="A1.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\overline{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\overline{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, RB-CBS effectively shifts its priority, reducing the number of steps taken to be highly competitive with the most path-efficient methods while maintaining a very high success rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The numerical data in Tables <a href="#A1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Point Environment ‣ A.2 More Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Risk-Bounded Multi-Agent Visual Navigation via Dynamic Budget Allocation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Point Environment ‣ A.2 More Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Risk-Bounded Multi-Agent Visual Navigation via Dynamic Budget Allocation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> corroborates these findings. For instance, in the Hard 10 agent scenario (Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Point Environment ‣ A.2 More Results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Risk-Bounded Multi-Agent Visual Navigation via Dynamic Budget Allocation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), our method improves its step count from 17.39 to 9.75 as <math id="A1.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> increases, a clear demonstration of its ability to leverage the available risk budget. Furthermore, its success rate remains robust (e.g., 84-96% for <math id="A1.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\geq 25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\geq 25\%</annotation></semantics></math>) where other methods like Lagrangian Search can be brittle. This confirms that RB-CBS provides a reliable and adaptable solution for multi-agent navigation, effectively balancing user-specified risk with path efficiency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08157v1/pointenv_easy_5_svg-tex.svg" id="A1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/206;" width="698" height="206"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08157v1/pointenv_easy_10_svg-tex.svg" id="A1.F8.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/206;" width="698" height="206"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08157v1/pointenv_medium_5_svg-tex.svg" id="A1.F8.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/206;" width="698" height="206"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08157v1/pointenv_medium_10_svg-tex.svg" id="A1.F8.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/206;" width="698" height="206"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>
Quantitative results for the 2D Point environment comparing all methods across Easy and Medium difficulties for 5 and 10 agents. These plots visually demonstrate the trade-offs between success rate, steps, and cumulative cost as the risk budget (<math id="A1.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>) is varied. Our method, RB-CBS (purple), consistently shows a strong adaptive profile.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08157v1/pointenv_hard_5_svg-tex.svg" id="A1.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/206;" width="698" height="206"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>
Quantitative results for the 2D Point environment comparing all methods across Hard difficulty for 5 agents. These plots again visually demonstrate the trade-offs between success rate, steps, and cumulative cost as the risk budget (<math id="A1.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>) is varied. Our method, RB-CBS (purple), consistently shows a strong adaptive profile.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure ltx_flex_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A1.T2.tab1" class="ltx_table ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<table id="A1.T2.tab1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A1.T2.tab1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
